    "seo": {
      "title": "Graduate Employment in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iran’s transition from university to work, including graduate unemployment, participation, skills mismatches, and evidence gaps."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "transition-to-work",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The transition from university to work",
        "content": "A university qualification and a suitable job are different achievements. Iran’s experience illustrates why expanding education must be examined alongside the economy’s capacity to create work. The World Bank’s Spring 2021 assessment described longstanding problems in absorbing educated young people, rather than a difficulty confined to the pandemic. It also noted that continued education had previously delayed labour-market entry for part of the large generation born during the 1980s. This is a historical explanation of labour supply, not a description of every graduate’s experience. [s1]\n\nThe same assessment found that much of the employment expansion before the pandemic involved self-employment and very small service businesses. A rising employment total therefore did not necessarily mean a comparable expansion in stable professional positions. For graduates, whether work uses their education is a separate question from whether they count as employed. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-statistics",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What unemployment figures establish",
        "content": "The World Bank reported graduate unemployment of 11.8 percent in the Iranian reporting year labelled 2023/24. This is a dated national observation, not a verified rate for the research cutoff. Its discussion identifies insufficient job creation and low participation as continuing problems, especially for women, young people, and university graduates. [s2]\n\nAn unemployment rate needs its denominator. The labour force consists of people classified as employed or unemployed; it excludes people outside those categories. Consequently, graduate unemployment should not be read as the proportion of all degree holders without jobs. Someone continuing education or no longer seeking work may fall outside the labour force. [s3]\n\nA second distinction concerns composition: the share of unemployed people who hold degrees answers a different question from the unemployment rate among graduates. Neither measure alone establishes how long a new graduate waits for work, whether the job matches the degree, or whether earnings justify the cost of education. Those questions require additional evidence.",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "skills-and-demand",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Skills are only one part of the explanation",
        "content": "UNCTAD’s innovation-policy review identified a mismatch between education, professional training, and industry requirements. It also described weak incentives and deficient collaboration for turning scientific capabilities into commercial innovation. Read together, these findings suggest that the transition problem cannot be reduced to graduates choosing the wrong subjects: the organisation of production and demand for skilled work also matter. This is an interpretation of a historical review, not a measured allocation of causes in today’s labour market. [s4]\n\nA fuller account would compare outcomes by qualification, subject, gender, location, and time since graduation. It would also separate finding any work from obtaining secure, adequately paid work that uses university-level skills. Until comparable evidence is assembled, a national headline should remain a starting point for understanding graduate employment rather than a verdict on the value of an individual degree.",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      }
