    "description": "Iran’s pandemic response made digital teaching and educational broadcasting central to school continuity. Its experience highlights the difference between providing a platform and ensuring that learners can access, use, and benefit from it. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["Online education", "Digital inclusion", "SHAD", "Accessibility", "Student privacy"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T18:08:55Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T18:08:55Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Online Education in Iran: Access and Learning | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore SHAD, pandemic teaching, accessibility, and privacy, with clear distinctions between platform reach and learning outcomes."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "school-continuity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An emergency shift in how school worked",
        "content": "When pandemic closures interrupted classroom teaching, Iran used both broadcasting and internet-based education. UNESCO’s Institute for Information Technologies in Education describes televised lessons beginning in March 2020 and the Ministry of Education’s introduction of SHAD, a student education network. SHAD reproduced familiar school arrangements online: pupils joined virtual classrooms, principals organised classes, and staff monitored attendance and teaching activity. [s1]\n\nThis matters because emergency remote teaching is more than publishing lessons on a website. It relocates communication, assignments, and the routines of school into a mediated environment. Iran’s response sought continuity through an organised school network, while television offered a different delivery channel. Neither the availability of a broadcast nor access to a virtual classroom establishes how much a particular pupil learned. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "access-and-accessibility",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Participation requires more than a connection",
        "content": "UNESCO’s account records criticism of SHAD’s technical performance and the exclusion of pupils without suitable devices or internet access. These are distinct obstacles: a household may have connectivity but no device available when a lesson takes place, while an installed application may still be difficult to use. The historical account should not be treated as a technical audit of the platform’s current version. [s1]\n\nDisability introduces another dimension. In the publicly accessible abstract of their qualitative study, Negin Hosseini Goodrich and Abouzar Samiei argued that the pandemic shift transferred existing physical and attitudinal barriers into online education. Their study specifically addressed students with disabilities and described SHAD as inaccessible in that context. The abstract supports identifying accessibility as a serious concern; it does not provide a national prevalence estimate or establish the condition of later releases. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "privacy-and-choice",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Privacy is part of educational provision",
        "content": "Human Rights Watch’s investigation of government-endorsed educational technology included testimony from Iranian teachers who said public-school staff were compelled to use SHAD during the pandemic. That reported lack of choice matters when assessing meaningful consent to digital systems. The report’s technical investigations were snapshots from 2021; they should not be presented as proof of how software behaves today. [s3]\n\nA careful evaluation therefore asks separate questions about educational usefulness, accessibility, and data handling. A useful lesson does not by itself answer what information is collected, who receives it, or whether a learner can participate through an alternative channel. These are evaluation questions, not findings that every Iranian platform has the same practices. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "inclusive-learning",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From emergency provision to inclusive learning",
        "content": "UNICEF’s Iran education programme identifies economic and geographical barriers, shortages of ICT equipment, and pressure on schools serving refugee communities. Its stated interventions include inclusive digital learning platforms and digital textbooks for children with disabilities and refugee children. These statements document programme priorities, rather than independently measured improvements in learning. [s4]\n\nThe central distinction is between reach and educational benefit. Evaluating online education requires evidence about who participates, who is excluded, what support teachers and families need, and what learners achieve. This page’s evidence is strongest on pandemic school provision. University teaching, adult learning, and the subsequent effectiveness of blended education require further research.",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      }
    ],
