    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran’s innovation landscape combines scientific and technical capabilities with difficult conditions for commercial development. This directory distinguishes the technologies and services businesses provide from the financing, infrastructure, and institutional conditions needed to sustain them. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["Innovation", "Enterprise software", "Digital services", "Entrepreneurship", "Technology", "Commercialisation"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T18:08:55Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T18:08:55Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Innovation & Business Opportunities in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Navigate Iran’s technology and business fields, from enterprise software and financial systems to applied innovation and entrepreneurship."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "capability-and-commercialisation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From technical capability to a working business",
        "content": "Innovation becomes economically significant when knowledge can be turned into useful products, reliable services, or better ways of producing them. Iran’s experience makes the distinction between scientific capability and commercial development particularly important. UNCTAD’s historical innovation-policy review described substantial human-capital and scientific assets alongside weaknesses in incentives, competition, and collaboration directed toward market demand. Its account is evidence about the development of the innovation system, not a current census of firms. [s1]\n\nThe World Intellectual Property Organization’s 2025 profile offers a more recent, differently constructed view. It placed Iran’s innovation outputs ahead of its innovation inputs in relative international ranking. That pattern is consistent with a system producing identifiable knowledge and technology outputs despite weaker enabling conditions. It does not mean every business is productive, every research result reaches customers, or a prospective investment will succeed. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "software-and-finance",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Software foundations and financial systems",
        "content": "This directory begins with distinctions that help readers follow a product from its underlying technology to its business use. Software Development Infrastructure covers the organisations and capabilities involved in building enterprise and custom applications. Core Banking concerns the central systems behind bank accounts and transactions. Banking & Financial Systems broadens that view to functions such as lending, treasury, branch operations, customer management, and control.\n\nElectronic Payments and Financial Technology overlap, but answer different editorial questions. The payments branch follows acceptance, gateways, switches, and transaction management. Financial Technology also includes areas such as digital credit assessment, investment services, crowdfunding, and open banking—the controlled exchange of banking functions or data through interfaces. Insurance Technology follows a separate set of processes, including policy administration, risk assessment, claims, and distribution.\n\nThese are navigation boundaries for this guide. They do not imply that a provider operates in only one branch, or that the presence of a technology establishes regulatory permission, adoption, or service quality.",
        "source_ids": []
      },
      {
        "id": "platforms-and-operations",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Customer platforms and the systems behind them",
        "content": "E-commerce and Online Services focus on the customer-facing side of digital business: selling goods, matching participants, and arranging services. Cloud Infrastructure and Software Delivery & Operations examine the supporting systems. They ask how applications are hosted, released, monitored, secured, and kept available. A marketplace interface and the operational infrastructure beneath it are related subjects, but they require different evidence.\n\nArtificial Intelligence, Data Science, and Cybersecurity are treated as cross-cutting capabilities. Their branch pages distinguish language and speech technologies, predictive analysis, business intelligence, identity management, and security controls. The useful editorial question is what a system demonstrably does for its users. A product description alone cannot establish accuracy, robustness, or operational effectiveness.\n\nBlockchain Technology likewise separates infrastructure, custody, wallets, monitoring, and smart contracts. Inclusion in the taxonomy is a way to explain these activities; it is not an endorsement of particular assets or a statement about their legal treatment.",
        "source_ids": []
      },
      {
        "id": "applied-innovation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Applied technology across economic sectors",
        "content": "The applied branches organise technology around the problems it is intended to address. Health Technology follows clinical information and service administration. Education Technology covers learning delivery and assessment. Agricultural Technology includes monitoring, irrigation management, and supply chains, while Industrial Technology examines production systems and automation. Transport Technology follows fleets, routing, freight, and logistics.\n\nGaming & Digital Entertainment introduces another kind of product: interactive cultural and entertainment experiences. Emerging Businesses groups developing approaches such as business-process automation, specialist marketplaces, and subscription services. Across these fields, the distinction between an announced capability, a deployed system, and a measured outcome remains essential.\n\nEntrepreneurship & Investment explains mechanisms for establishing and financing businesses. Technology Exports asks how products and technical services reach customers beyond Iran. These branches complement the sector pages rather than substituting for evidence about individual companies.",
        "source_ids": []
      },
      {
        "id": "operating-conditions",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why operating conditions matter",
        "content": "Technology does not remove the economic conditions under which a business operates. The World Bank’s Spring 2024 assessment identified sanctions, energy shortages, liquidity constraints, underinvestment, and uncertainty as impediments to non-oil activity. It also described banking restrictions and trade barriers as obstacles to technology transfer and competitiveness. These are dated findings; the report’s forecasts should not be read as observed outcomes at this page’s cutoff. [s3]\n\nUNCTAD’s earlier diagnosis also emphasised the gap between scientific strengths and demand-led commercial innovation. Together, these sources support looking beyond the supply of engineers or the number of research outputs. They do not establish the relative importance of each constraint for a particular business today. [s1]\n\nRegional generalisations require similar care. A national innovation profile cannot establish that firms in different cities have equivalent access to finance, specialist workers, infrastructure, or customers. This draft does not yet contain a verified regional comparison.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-innovation-indicators",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What an innovation index can show",
        "content": "WIPO’s 2025 country profile grouped indicators into innovation inputs and outputs, while explicitly warning that data availability and changes to its framework affect year-to-year ranking comparisons. The historical positions are therefore retained in a table rather than presented as a continuous measure of business opportunity. A change in rank is not a percentage change in innovative capacity. [s2]\n\nWIPO’s subsequently updated country page contains a 2026 edition. It is listed as a research lead, but its release date relative to this page’s cutoff was not established during inspection, so its results are excluded here. [s4]\n\nFor readers exploring the directory, the strongest approach is to move from a defined activity to inspected evidence: what is offered, who uses it, how performance is measured, and which limitations remain. The branches provide that structure; company-specific opportunities still require current research.",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "innovation-ranking-table",
        "type": "table",
        "content": {
          "title": "Historical GII positions reported in WIPO’s 2025 Iran profile; annual ranks are not a constant-method performance series",
          "dataset_id": "gii-historical-positions"
        },
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [
      {
        "id": "gii-historical-positions",
